SECURITY RECEIPTS

This may be the best portion of the case.

Receipt 01 — Initial Company Position

McGraw Hill described:

unauthorized access to a limited set of data from a Salesforce-hosted webpage.

The company further said its internal systems, Salesforce accounts, courseware, and customer databases were not accessed.

InfinityGrid evidence classification:

E4 — McGraw Hill officially made the statement.

Important distinction:

That proves what McGraw Hill reported, not necessarily that every aspect of the eventual dataset had already been independently quantified.

Receipt 02 — Attacker Position

ShinyHunters claimed:

~45 million Salesforce records

and threatened publication after its extortion demand was not satisfied.

InfinityGrid:

E1 — CLAIMED

Do not turn this into 45M “victims.”

Receipt 03 — Public Dataset

Then comes the strongest independent evidence.

More than 100 GB of material was publicly distributed, and HIBP found 13.5 million unique email addresses, together with inconsistent occurrences of names, physical addresses, and phone numbers.

That moves the case from:

ATTACKER SAYS DATA EXISTS

to:

PUBLIC DATASET EXISTS
        ↓
INDEPENDENT ANALYSIS
        ↓
13.5M UNIQUE EMAILS

That's an enormous evidentiary difference.

Receipt 04 — The Scope Language

Now line up the three versions:

MCGRAW HILL
"Limited set of data"


        ↓


SHINYHUNTERS
~45 million records


        ↓


INDEPENDENTLY ANALYZED DATA
100+ GB
13.5 million unique emails
plus names / phones / addresses

InfinityGrid does not need to write:

McGraw Hill lied.

Your stronger statement is:

The later public dataset materially improves the public's ability to measure the incident beyond either the company's initial qualitative description or the attacker's raw record-count claim.

That's devastatingly effective because it's also defensible.
